  "final_analysis": "The clinical presentation and laboratory findings in this 4-year-old boy suggest a diagnosis of nephrotic syndrome, characterized by significant proteinuria, hypoalbuminemia, and edema. The history of a recent sore throat followed by these symptoms raises the possibility of post-infectious glomerulonephritis; however, the normal serum complement levels and absence of hematuria are more consistent with minimal change disease, a common cause of nephrotic syndrome in children. Prednisone therapy is the standard initial treatment for nephrotic syndrome in children, particularly for minimal change disease, which responds well to corticosteroids. This treatment aims to reduce proteinuria and manage edema through its anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressive effects. Other options such as enalapril (an ACE inhibitor) and cyclosporine (an immunosuppressant) are more commonly used in cases resistant to corticosteroids or where steroid-sparing agents are needed. Furosemide (a diuretic) may be used to manage symptoms of fluid overload but does not address the underlying disease. Testing for anti-streptolysin O levels would be more relevant if post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis were suspected, which is not indicated here due to the clinical and laboratory profile. Therefore, prednisone therapy is the most appropriate and consensus-supported next step in management.",
